\cf0 \strokec2 The Master's in Informatics at TUM is a 120 ECTS, four-semester program emphasizing research-oriented depth in computer science, with flexible specializations and profiles (e.g., research via guided projects or practice-focused practicums). As of January 2026, regulations and guidelines ensure transparent credit recognition for prior achievements, international mobility, or parallel studies, adhering to the Bologna Process, Lisbon Recognition Convention (LRC), and Bavarian Higher Education Act (BayHSchG). Recognition decisions hinge on demonstrating no "substantial differences" in learning outcomes\'97measurable skills and knowledge\'97rather than rigid equivalence in credits or content. This approach reverses the burden of proof: TUM must justify any rejections in writing, allowing appeals within one month or via administrative courts. Processes are program-specific, with Informatics handled through the CIT school's examination board, prioritizing mobility and study efficiency without retroactive approvals for completed degrees or failed TUM exams.\
The curriculum, updated for WS 2025/26, requires 53 elective credits (one specialization with \uc0\u8805 18 ECTS, two supplements with \u8805 8 ECTS each, plus 9 free), at least 10 theory credits, 6 support electives (e.g., soft skills), a 10 ECTS practicum, 5 ECTS seminar, 16 ECTS interdisciplinary project, and a 30 ECTS Master's thesis. Restrictions apply, such as limiting certain module groups (e.g., only one from Machine Learning basics). Part-time options (50% or 66% load) extend durations to 8 or 6 semesters with adjusted deadlines. Eligibility demands a Bachelor's with \u8805 50 Informatics fundamentals credits, English proficiency, and an aptitude assessment (scoring on grades, essays, tests like GRE/GATE).\
Credit recognition applications must occur post-enrollment but within the first year (or two semesters), using forms submitted to designated emails. Required documents include module descriptions (detailing outcomes, content, workload), transcripts, certificates, and grading schemes\'97translated if needed, but no handwritten items. For international mobility (e.g., Erasmus+), pre-mobility Learning Agreements (via OLA platform) ensure provisional approval, with post-mobility transfers based on actual achievements. Minimums for recognition include 20 ECTS/semester abroad (\uc0\u8805 10 in Informatics), with codes for components and provisions for changes. Elective caps apply if exceeded, and grades convert via the Bavarian Formula for non-compatible systems, excluding pass/fail from averages.\
Rejections are rare and must detail specific gaps (e.g., cognitive levels from remember to create per Anderson/Krathwohl taxonomy). Partial recognition or supplements (e.g., exams for missing competencies) are common alternatives. Pre-consultations with advisors are recommended, and decisions are documented as precedents for consistency. Data privacy follows GDPR, with raw data preserved without AI manipulation per legal standards. For Garching/Heilbronn splits, campus-specific nuances may apply, but core rules align.\
